{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ULphXGzAsoRcAhRS5Thvet","type":"tool_result","content":"# Encrypting Confidential Data at Rest in Kubernetes\n\n## Introduction\n\nAll of the APIs in Kubernetes that let you write persistent API resource data support at-rest encryption. For example, you can enable at-rest encryption for Secrets. This at-rest encryption is additional to any system-level encryption for the etcd cluster or for the filesystem(s) on hosts where you are running the kube-apiserver.\n\nBy default, the API server stores plain-text representations of resources into etcd, with no at-rest encryption.\n\n## Determining Whether Encryption at Rest Is Already Enabled\n\nThe `kube-apiserver` process accepts an argument `--encryption-provider-config` that specifies a path to a configuration file. The contents of that file, if you specify one, control how Kubernetes API data is encrypted in etcd. If you are running the kube-apiserver without the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, you do not have encryption at rest enabled. If you are running the kube-apiserver with the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, and the file that it references specifies the `identity` provider as the first encryption provider in the list, then you do not have at-rest encryption enabled (**the default `identity` provider does not provide any confidentiality protection.**).\n\nIf you are running the kube-apiserver with the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, and the file that it references specifies a provider other than `identity` as the first encryption provider in the list, then you already have at-rest encryption enabled.\n\n## Understanding the Encryption at Rest Configuration\n\n### EncryptionConfiguration Resource\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\nkind: EncryptionConfiguration\nresources:\n  - resources:\n      - secrets\n    providers:\n      - aescbc:\n          keys:\n            - name: key1\n              secret: <base64-encoded-key>\n```\n\n### Available Providers\n\nThe `providers` array is an ordered list of the possible encryption providers. Each provider supports multiple keys—the keys are tried in order for decryption, and if the provider is the first provider, the first key is used for encryption.\n\n| Provider | Strength | Notes |\n|----------|----------|-------|\n| `identity` | None (plain text) | Does not provide confidentiality; stores resources unencrypted |\n| `aescbc` | Strong | AES-CBC encryption with PKCS#7 padding |\n| `aesgcm` | Strong | AES-GCM encryption; recommended over aescbc |\n| `secretbox` | Strong | XSalsa20 and Poly1305 encryption |\n| `kms` (v1) | Strongest | Uses external Key Management Service |\n| `kms` (v2) | Strongest | Uses external Key Management Service (improved version) |\n\n### Key Provider Usage\n\nOnly one provider type may be specified per entry (`identity` or `aescbc` may be provided, but not both in the same item). **The first provider in the list is used to encrypt resources written into the storage.** When reading resources from storage, each provider that matches the stored data attempts in order to decrypt the data. If no provider can read the stored data due to a mismatch in format or secret key, an error is returned which prevents clients from accessing that resource.\n\n### Important Warnings\n\n#### Caution: The Identity Provider\n\nThe `identity` provider does not encrypt data and should not be used as your primary encryption mechanism. If the `identity` provider is listed first in your configuration, your data will not be encrypted at rest.\n\n#### Caution: Configuration File Storage\n\nYour encryption configuration file contains encryption keys and should be stored securely. Restrict access to the encryption configuration file and ensure it is protected at rest with appropriate file permissions.\n\n## Encrypt All Secrets\n\nWhen you enable encryption, only newly written secrets are encrypted. To ensure all secrets are encrypted, you must read all secrets through the API server and write them back. This forces them to be encrypted with the configured encryption provider.\n\n## Verify That the Data Is Encrypted\n\nAfter enabling encryption, you can verify that newly written data is being encrypted by checking the etcd database directly. Encrypted data will appear as binary data rather than plain text when inspected.\n\n## What Encryption at Rest Protects and Does Not Protect\n\n**Encryption at rest protects:**\n- API resource data stored in etcd from being readable if an attacker gains direct access to the etcd database storage\n\n**Encryption at rest does NOT protect:**\n- Data from a caller with API read access—if someone can read Secrets via the Kubernetes API, they can access the unencrypted secret values\n- Data in flight (use TLS for that)\n- Data in memory within the API server process"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"5b8eebcb-c534-4cba-aed7-341967e04253","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:22:56.389Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":557724,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Encrypting Confidential Data at Rest in Kubernetes\n\n## Introduction\n\nAll of the APIs in Kubernetes that let you write persistent API resource data support at-rest encryption. For example, you can enable at-rest encryption for Secrets. This at-rest encryption is additional to any system-level encryption for the etcd cluster or for the filesystem(s) on hosts where you are running the kube-apiserver.\n\nBy default, the API server stores plain-text representations of resources into etcd, with no at-rest encryption.\n\n## Determining Whether Encryption at Rest Is Already Enabled\n\nThe `kube-apiserver` process accepts an argument `--encryption-provider-config` that specifies a path to a configuration file. The contents of that file, if you specify one, control how Kubernetes API data is encrypted in etcd. If you are running the kube-apiserver without the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, you do not have encryption at rest enabled. If you are running the kube-apiserver with the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, and the file that it references specifies the `identity` provider as the first encryption provider in the list, then you do not have at-rest encryption enabled (**the default `identity` provider does not provide any confidentiality protection.**).\n\nIf you are running the kube-apiserver with the `--encryption-provider-config` command line argument, and the file that it references specifies a provider other than `identity` as the first encryption provider in the list, then you already have at-rest encryption enabled.\n\n## Understanding the Encryption at Rest Configuration\n\n### EncryptionConfiguration Resource\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\nkind: EncryptionConfiguration\nresources:\n  - resources:\n      - secrets\n    providers:\n      - aescbc:\n          keys:\n            - name: key1\n              secret: <base64-encoded-key>\n```\n\n### Available Providers\n\nThe `providers` array is an ordered list of the possible encryption providers. Each provider supports multiple keys—the keys are tried in order for decryption, and if the provider is the first provider, the first key is used for encryption.\n\n| Provider | Strength | Notes |\n|----------|----------|-------|\n| `identity` | None (plain text) | Does not provide confidentiality; stores resources unencrypted |\n| `aescbc` | Strong | AES-CBC encryption with PKCS#7 padding |\n| `aesgcm` | Strong | AES-GCM encryption; recommended over aescbc |\n| `secretbox` | Strong | XSalsa20 and Poly1305 encryption |\n| `kms` (v1) | Strongest | Uses external Key Management Service |\n| `kms` (v2) | Strongest | Uses external Key Management Service (improved version) |\n\n### Key Provider Usage\n\nOnly one provider type may be specified per entry (`identity` or `aescbc` may be provided, but not both in the same item). **The first provider in the list is used to encrypt resources written into the storage.** When reading resources from storage, each provider that matches the stored data attempts in order to decrypt the data. If no provider can read the stored data due to a mismatch in format or secret key, an error is returned which prevents clients from accessing that resource.\n\n### Important Warnings\n\n#### Caution: The Identity Provider\n\nThe `identity` provider does not encrypt data and should not be used as your primary encryption mechanism. If the `identity` provider is listed first in your configuration, your data will not be encrypted at rest.\n\n#### Caution: Configuration File Storage\n\nYour encryption configuration file contains encryption keys and should be stored securely. Restrict access to the encryption configuration file and ensure it is protected at rest with appropriate file permissions.\n\n## Encrypt All Secrets\n\nWhen you enable encryption, only newly written secrets are encrypted. To ensure all secrets are encrypted, you must read all secrets through the API server and write them back. This forces them to be encrypted with the configured encryption provider.\n\n## Verify That the Data Is Encrypted\n\nAfter enabling encryption, you can verify that newly written data is being encrypted by checking the etcd database directly. Encrypted data will appear as binary data rather than plain text when inspected.\n\n## What Encryption at Rest Protects and Does Not Protect\n\n**Encryption at rest protects:**\n- API resource data stored in etcd from being readable if an attacker gains direct access to the etcd database storage\n\n**Encryption at rest does NOT protect:**\n- Data from a caller with API read access—if someone can read Secrets via the Kubernetes API, they can access the unencrypted secret values\n- Data in flight (use TLS for that)\n- Data in memory within the API server process","durationMs":12691,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JdUw5nyfAuLZkjmLnjKNzK","type":"tool_result","content":"# Linux Kernel Security Constraints for Pods and Containers\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here are the key sections on Linux kernel security features:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe page describes security features built into the Linux kernel that you can use in Kubernetes workloads. It covers how to apply these features to Pods and containers using the Pod specification's `securityContext` field.\n\n## Running Workloads Without Root Privileges\n\nThe documentation recommends using the Pod `securityContext` to define specific Linux users and groups for processes, explicitly restricting containers from running as root. The Pod manifest specification takes precedence over similar values in the container image.\n\n## Security Features in the Linux Kernel\n\nKubernetes supports three main Linux kernel security features:\n\n- **Secure computing mode (seccomp)**: Filters which system calls a process can make\n- **AppArmor**: Restricts access privileges of individual programs\n- **Security Enhanced Linux (SELinux)**: Assigns security labels to objects for security policy enforcement\n\nThese are configured through the `securityContext` field in Pod specifications.\n\n### seccomp\n\nseccomp restricts individual syscalls by filtering which system calls a process can make. Linux capabilities divide privileges into categories so processes get only needed privileges. Each container runtime includes a default seccomp profile, and Kubernetes allows automatically applying seccomp profiles to Pods and containers.\n\nThe `allowPrivilegeEscalation` setting prevents processes from gaining new capabilities when set to `false`.\n\n**Recommendation**: Use the default seccomp profile bundled with your container runtime rather than custom configurations, which can break during updates and become difficult to manage at scale.\n\n### AppArmor and SELinux\n\n**AppArmor** is a Linux security module that confines programs to limited resources through profiles tuned for specific access needs (capabilities, network access, file permissions). Profiles can run in enforcing mode (blocks disallowed access) or complain mode (reports violations only).\n\n**SELinux** is a kernel security module that restricts access using security policies applied to labeled subjects (processes) and resources (files). In Kubernetes, you set SELinux labels in the `securityContext` field.\n\n**Key differences**:\n- AppArmor uses file paths; SELinux uses inode indexing\n- AppArmor uses profiles; SELinux uses labeled policies"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"847b6525-97f5-4a8a-89a8-6007c6feb994","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:23:00.102Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":509227,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Linux Kernel Security Constraints for Pods and Containers\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here are the key sections on Linux kernel security features:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe page describes security features built into the Linux kernel that you can use in Kubernetes workloads. It covers how to apply these features to Pods and containers using the Pod specification's `securityContext` field.\n\n## Running Workloads Without Root Privileges\n\nThe documentation recommends using the Pod `securityContext` to define specific Linux users and groups for processes, explicitly restricting containers from running as root. The Pod manifest specification takes precedence over similar values in the container image.\n\n## Security Features in the Linux Kernel\n\nKubernetes supports three main Linux kernel security features:\n\n- **Secure computing mode (seccomp)**: Filters which system calls a process can make\n- **AppArmor**: Restricts access privileges of individual programs\n- **Security Enhanced Linux (SELinux)**: Assigns security labels to objects for security policy enforcement\n\nThese are configured through the `securityContext` field in Pod specifications.\n\n### seccomp\n\nseccomp restricts individual syscalls by filtering which system calls a process can make. Linux capabilities divide privileges into categories so processes get only needed privileges. Each container runtime includes a default seccomp profile, and Kubernetes allows automatically applying seccomp profiles to Pods and containers.\n\nThe `allowPrivilegeEscalation` setting prevents processes from gaining new capabilities when set to `false`.\n\n**Recommendation**: Use the default seccomp profile bundled with your container runtime rather than custom configurations, which can break during updates and become difficult to manage at scale.\n\n### AppArmor and SELinux\n\n**AppArmor** is a Linux security module that confines programs to limited resources through profiles tuned for specific access needs (capabilities, network access, file permissions). Profiles can run in enforcing mode (blocks disallowed access) or complain mode (reports violations only).\n\n**SELinux** is a kernel security module that restricts access using security policies applied to labeled subjects (processes) and resources (files). In Kubernetes, you set SELinux labels in the `securityContext` field.\n\n**Key differences**:\n- AppArmor uses file paths; SELinux uses inode indexing\n- AppArmor uses profiles; SELinux uses labeled policies","durationMs":8426,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/linux-kernel-security-constraints/"}}
