 security are Cloud, Clusters, Containers, and Code. This approach in layers increases the defense in depth of security, which is considered a good practice in security for systems software.\n\n**Key Information:**\n\nThe search found the official Kubernetes documentation at:\n- **https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/** (the main Cloud Native Security page)\n- **https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/overview/** (security overview page)\n\nBoth pages are part of the official Kubernetes documentation and cover the 4C's security model along with specific recommendations for protecting infrastructure, clusters, containers, and code."],"durationSeconds":5.232783599999995,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017M1zuQNQafh6g4BioGEbkR","type":"tool_result","content":"# Response to Content Extraction Request\n\n## (a) First three paragraphs of body prose after front matter:\n\n\"All of the APIs in Kubernetes that let you write persistent API resource data support at-rest encryption. For example, you can enable at-rest encryption for Secrets. This at-rest encryption is additional to any system-level encryption for the etcd cluster or for the filesystem(s) on hosts where you are running the kube-apiserver.\n\nThis page shows how to enable and configure encryption of API data at rest.\n\nThis task covers encryption for resource data stored using the Kubernetes API. For example, you can encrypt Secret objects, including the key-value data they contain.\"\n\n## (b) Complete \"Providers for Kubernetes encryption at rest\" table:\n\nNOT PRESENT (The document contains a table with this caption, but the actual table structure uses HTML with `<table>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>` tags rather than markdown formatting. The rows cover: identity, aescbc, aesgcm, kms v1 (deprecated), kms v2, and secretbox providers with columns for Name, Encryption, Strength, Speed, and Key length.)\n\n## (c) Every Caution, Warning and Note callout:\n\n**Note 1:** \"This task covers encryption for resource data stored using the Kubernetes API...If you want to encrypt data in filesystems that are mounted into containers, you instead need to either: use a storage integration that provides encrypted volumes [or] encrypt the data within your own application\"\n\n**Caution 1:** \"If any resource is not readable via the encryption configuration (because keys were changed), and you cannot restore a working configuration, your only recourse is to delete that entry from the underlying etcd directly.\"\n\n**Caution 2:** \"Storing the raw encryption key in the EncryptionConfig only moderately improves your security posture, compared to no encryption.\"\n\n**Caution 3:** \"The encryption configuration file may contain keys that can decrypt content in etcd. If the configuration file contains any key material, you must properly restrict permissions on all your control plane hosts so only the user who runs the kube-apiserver can read this configuration.\"\n\n**Caution 4:** \"Your config file contains keys that can decrypt the contents in etcd, so you must properly restrict permissions on your control-plane nodes so only the user who runs the kube-apiserver can read it.\"\n\n**Caution 5:** \"For cluster configurations with two or more control plane nodes, the encryption configuration should be identical across each control plane node.\"\n\n**Warning:** \"Making this change prevents the API server from retrieving resources that are marked as encrypted at rest, but are actually stored in the clear. When you have configured encryption at rest for an API (for example: the API kind Secret, representing secrets resources in the core API group), you must ensure that all those resources in this cluster really are encrypted at rest.\"\n\n**Note 2:** \"Keep the encryption key confidential, including while you generate it and ideally even after you are no longer actively using it.\"\n\n**Note 3:** \"Use of wildcards that overlap within the same resource list or across multiple entries are not allowed since part of the configuration would be ineffective.\"\n\n**Note 4:** \"If an error occurs due to a conflicting write, retry the command. It is safe to run that command more than once. For larger clusters, you may wish to subdivide the Secrets by namespace, or script an update.\"\n\n## (d) Paragraph beginning \"The first provider in the list is used to encrypt\":\n\n\"The first provider in the list is used to encrypt resources written into the storage. When reading resources from storage, each provider that matches the stored data attempts in order to decrypt the data. If no provider can read the stored data due to a mismatch in format or secret key, an error is returned which prevents clients from accessing that resource.\"\n\n## (e) Prose under \"Ensure all relevant data are encrypted\" and \"Verify that data is encrypted\":\n\n**Ensure all relevant data are encrypted section:** \"It's often not enough to make sure that new objects get encrypted: you also want that encryption to apply to the objects that are already stored. For this example, you have configured your cluster so that Secrets are encrypted on write. Performing a replace operation for each Secret will encrypt that content at rest, where the objects are unchanged. You can make this change across all Secrets in your cluster: `kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -` The command above reads all Secrets and then updates them with the same data, in order to apply server side encryption.\"\n\n**Verify that data is encrypted section:** \"Data is encrypted when written to etcd. After restarting your kube-apiserver, any newly created or updated Secret (or other resource kinds configured in EncryptionConfiguration) should be encrypted when stored. To check this, you can use the etcdctl command line program to retrieve the contents of your secret data. This example shows how to check this for encrypting the Secret API.\" [Followed by numbered steps 1-4 with shell commands and verification instructions.]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"1261f0c1-9855-460c-a9ae-1e229188472b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:25:25.013Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":30336,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Response to Content Extraction Request\n\n## (a) First three paragraphs of body prose after front matter:\n\n\"All of the APIs in Kubernetes that let you write persistent API resource data support at-rest encryption. For example, you can enable at-rest encryption for Secrets. This at-rest encryption is additional to any system-level encryption for the etcd cluster or for the filesystem(s) on hosts where you are running the kube-apiserver.\n\nThis page shows how to enable and configure encryption of API data at rest.\n\nThis task covers encryption for resource data stored using the Kubernetes API. For example, you can encrypt Secret objects, including the key-value data they contain.\"\n\n## (b) Complete \"Providers for Kubernetes encryption at rest\" table:\n\nNOT PRESENT (The document contains a table with this caption, but the actual table structure uses HTML with `<table>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>` tags rather than markdown formatting. The rows cover: identity, aescbc, aesgcm, kms v1 (deprecated), kms v2, and secretbox providers with columns for Name, Encryption, Strength, Speed, and Key length.)\n\n## (c) Every Caution, Warning and Note callout:\n\n**Note 1:** \"This task covers encryption for resource data stored using the Kubernetes API...If you want to encrypt data in filesystems that are mounted into containers, you instead need to either: use a storage integration that provides encrypted volumes [or] encrypt the data within your own application\"\n\n**Caution 1:** \"If any resource is not readable via the encryption configuration (because keys were changed), and you cannot restore a working configuration, your only recourse is to delete that entry from the underlying etcd directly.\"\n\n**Caution 2:** \"Storing the raw encryption key in the EncryptionConfig only moderately improves your security posture, compared to no encryption.\"\n\n**Caution 3:** \"The encryption configuration file may contain keys that can decrypt content in etcd. If the configuration file contains any key material, you must properly restrict permissions on all your control plane hosts so only the user who runs the kube-apiserver can read this configuration.\"\n\n**Caution 4:** \"Your config file contains keys that can decrypt the contents in etcd, so you must properly restrict permissions on your control-plane nodes so only the user who runs the kube-apiserver can read it.\"\n\n**Caution 5:** \"For cluster configurations with two or more control plane nodes, the encryption configuration should be identical across each control plane node.\"\n\n**Warning:** \"Making this change prevents the API server from retrieving resources that are marked as encrypted at rest, but are actually stored in the clear. When you have configured encryption at rest for an API (for example: the API kind Secret, representing secrets resources in the core API group), you must ensure that all those resources in this cluster really are encrypted at rest.\"\n\n**Note 2:** \"Keep the encryption key confidential, including while you generate it and ideally even after you are no longer actively using it.\"\n\n**Note 3:** \"Use of wildcards that overlap within the same resource list or across multiple entries are not allowed since part of the configuration would be ineffective.\"\n\n**Note 4:** \"If an error occurs due to a conflicting write, retry the command. It is safe to run that command more than once. For larger clusters, you may wish to subdivide the Secrets by namespace, or script an update.\"\n\n## (d) Paragraph beginning \"The first provider in the list is used to encrypt\":\n\n\"The first provider in the list is used to encrypt resources written into the storage. When reading resources from storage, each provider that matches the stored data attempts in order to decrypt the data. If no provider can read the stored data due to a mismatch in format or secret key, an error is returned which prevents clients from accessing that resource.\"\n\n## (e) Prose under \"Ensure all relevant data are encrypted\" and \"Verify that data is encrypted\":\n\n**Ensure all relevant data are encrypted section:** \"It's often not enough to make sure that new objects get encrypted: you also want that encryption to apply to the objects that are already stored. For this example, you have configured your cluster so that Secrets are encrypted