{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018WMn7yrARhWYzPN25X9ohn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Authorization Overview\n\nKubernetes authorization occurs after authentication and determines whether API requests should be allowed or denied. The system operates on a default-deny principle: \"All parts of an API request must be allowed by some authorization mechanism in order to proceed.\"\n\n## Authorization Decision Process\n\nThe API server evaluates all request attributes against configured policies. When multiple authorization modules are active, \"each is checked in sequence. If any authorizer approves or denies a request, that decision is immediately returned and no other authorizer is consulted. If all modules have no opinion on the request, then the request is denied.\"\n\n## Request Attributes Evaluated\n\nAuthorization decisions consider these API request attributes: user identity, group membership, extra metadata, whether the request targets an API resource, request path, API request verb (like get, list, create, update, patch, watch, delete, deletecollection), HTTP request verb, resource name/ID, subresource, namespace, and API group.\n\n## Authorization Modules\n\nKubernetes supports several authorization modes:\n\n**Node**: \"A special-purpose authorization mode that grants permissions to kubelets based on the pods they are scheduled to run.\"\n\n**ABAC**: \"Kubernetes ABAC mode defines an access control paradigm whereby access rights are granted to users through the use of policies which combine attributes together.\"\n\n**RBAC**: \"Kubernetes RBAC is a method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.\"\n\n**Webhook**: \"Kubernetes webhook mode for authorization makes a synchronous HTTP callout, blocking the request until the remote HTTP service responds to the query.\"\n\n## Checking Access with kubectl\n\nUsers can verify authorization status using `kubectl auth can-i`. This command queries the authorization layer to determine if actions are permitted. The `--as` flag enables impersonation: administrators can check what other users or service accounts can perform, facilitating authorization audits and troubleshooting."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"723700f8-420f-437a-9f90-81b92d3726fd","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:27:23.137Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":24934,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Authorization Overview\n\nKubernetes authorization occurs after authentication and determines whether API requests should be allowed or denied. The system operates on a default-deny principle: \"All parts of an API request must be allowed by some authorization mechanism in order to proceed.\"\n\n## Authorization Decision Process\n\nThe API server evaluates all request attributes against configured policies. When multiple authorization modules are active, \"each is checked in sequence. If any authorizer approves or denies a request, that decision is immediately returned and no other authorizer is consulted. If all modules have no opinion on the request, then the request is denied.\"\n\n## Request Attributes Evaluated\n\nAuthorization decisions consider these API request attributes: user identity, group membership, extra metadata, whether the request targets an API resource, request path, API request verb (like get, list, create, update, patch, watch, delete, deletecollection), HTTP request verb, resource name/ID, subresource, namespace, and API group.\n\n## Authorization Modules\n\nKubernetes supports several authorization modes:\n\n**Node**: \"A special-purpose authorization mode that grants permissions to kubelets based on the pods they are scheduled to run.\"\n\n**ABAC**: \"Kubernetes ABAC mode defines an access control paradigm whereby access rights are granted to users through the use of policies which combine attributes together.\"\n\n**RBAC**: \"Kubernetes RBAC is a method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.\"\n\n**Webhook**: \"Kubernetes webhook mode for authorization makes a synchronous HTTP callout, blocking the request until the remote HTTP service responds to the query.\"\n\n## Checking Access with kubectl\n\nUsers can verify authorization status using `kubectl auth can-i`. This command queries the authorization layer to determine if actions are permitted. The `--as` flag enables impersonation: administrators can check what other users or service accounts can perform, facilitating authorization audits and troubleshooting.","durationMs":6707,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/authorization.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017wNgov9T3avdAC9q7tgYhG","type":"tool_result","content":"# RBAC Authorization: Key Concepts and Operations\n\n## Referring to Resources\n\nRBAC uses the same string representation as the Kubernetes API for resource names. Subresources are referenced using a slash delimiter—for example, `pods/log` allows access to pod logs. You can restrict access to specific resource instances through the `resourceNames` field.\n\nHowever, there's an important limitation: \"You cannot restrict **deletecollection** or top-level **create** requests by resource name.\" The `resourceNames` field works with subresources like `pods/exec`, but for list or watch operations, clients must include a matching `metadata.name` field selector.\n\nWildcards (`*`) can grant broad permissions across resources and verbs, though this approach risks unintended access to future resource types and subresources.\n\n## Aggregated ClusterRoles\n\nClusterRoles can aggregate rules from other ClusterRoles using an `aggregationRule` with label selectors. A control plane controller automatically populates the combined `rules` field based on matching ClusterRole objects.\n\nCritical warning: \"The control plane overwrites any values that you manually specify in the `rules` field of an aggregate ClusterRole.\" To modify aggregated rules, update the selected ClusterRole objects instead. Avoid including a `rules` field in aggregated ClusterRole manifests to prevent field manager conflicts.\n\n## Referring to Subjects\n\nBindings grant permissions to subjects—users, groups, or ServiceAccounts. Kubernetes represents usernames as strings (plain names, email-style, or numeric IDs). The `system:` prefix is reserved for system use and should not appear in custom user or group names.\n\nServiceAccounts follow a specific naming convention: usernames use `system:serviceaccount:` prefix, while group names use `system:serviceaccounts:` (plural).\n\n## Privilege Escalation Prevention and Bootstrapping\n\nTwo key restrictions prevent unauthorized privilege escalation:\n\n**Role creation/update**: Users can only create or modify roles if they possess all permissions the role grants (at the same scope) or have explicit `escalate` permission on roles/clusterroles in the `rbac.authorization.k8s.io` API group.\n\n**Role binding creation/update**: Users can only create bindings if they hold all permissions in the referenced role OR have explicit `bind` permission on that role.\n\n## Default Roles and Role Bindings\n\nAPI servers automatically create default ClusterRoles and ClusterRoleBindings, labeled with `kubernetes.io/bootstrapping=rbac-defaults`. At startup, the API server reconciles defaults, restoring missing permissions and subjects.\n\nTo disable auto-reconciliation, set the `rbac.authorization.kubernetes.io/autoupdate` annotation to `false` on default cluster roles or bindings, though this risks non-functional clusters.\n\n### Discovery Roles Table\n\n| Default ClusterRole | Default ClusterRoleBinding | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **system:basic-user** | **system:authenticated** group | Provides read-only access to basic self-information |\n| **system:discovery** | **system:authenticated** group | Allows read-only access to API discovery endpoints |\n| **system:public-info-viewer** | **system:authenticated** and **system:unauthenticated** groups | Provides read-only access to non-sensitive cluster information |\n\n### User-Facing Roles Table\n\n| Default ClusterRole | Default ClusterRoleBinding | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **cluster-admin** | **system:masters** group | Grants super-user access to any action on any resource. In ClusterRoleBindings, provides full control over all cluster resources and namespaces. In RoleBindings, gives full control within the binding's namespace. |\n| **admin** | None | Intended for namespace-level use via RoleBinding. Allows read/write access to most namespace resources and enables creating roles and bindings within that namespace. Does not permit write access to resource quotas, the namespace itself, or EndpointSlices in Kubernetes v1.22+. |\n| **edit** | None | Permits read/write access to most namespace objects without viewing or modifying roles and bindings. Allows accessing Secrets and running Pods as any ServiceAccount in the namespace. Does not allow EndpointSlice write access in Kubernetes v1.22+. |\n| **view** | None | Provides read-only access to most namespace objects, excluding roles, bindings, and Secrets. Hiding Secrets prevents potential privilege escalation through ServiceAccount credential exposure. |\n\n## Command-Line Utilities\n\n### kubectl create role\n\nGenerates a Role within a single namespace with specified permissions. Examples include creating roles for pod reading, resource-specific access, API group specification, subresource permissions, and instance-level permissions.\n\n### kubectl create clusterrole\n\nCreates a ClusterRole for cluster-wide permissions. Capabilities include pod reader roles, resource name restrictions, API group specification, subresource access, non-resource URLs (like `/logs/*`), and aggregation rules.\n\n### kubectl create rolebinding\n\nGrants namespace-scoped permissions from a Role or ClusterRole to users or ServiceAccounts. Bindings apply within a specific namespace.\n\n### kubectl create clusterrolebinding\n\nExtends ClusterRole permissions across the entire cluster to users or ServiceAccounts.\n\n### kubectl auth reconcile\n\nCreates or updates RBAC objects from manifest files, handling namespace creation and updating existing roles/bindings. The `--remove-extra-permissions` and `--remove-extra-subjects` flags clean up unintended access. The `--dry-run=client` option previews changes before applying."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"06fdc24a-9ca4-4bca-bbb1-170b152902e9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:27:28.792Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":47888,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# RBAC Authorization: Key Concepts and Operations\n\n## Referring to Resources\n\nRBAC uses the same string representation as the Kubernetes API for resource names. Subresources are referenced using a slash delimiter—for example, `pods/log` allows access to pod logs. You can restrict access to specific resource instances through the `resourceNames` field.\n\nHowever, there's an important limitation: \"You cannot restrict **deletecollection** or top-level **create** requests by resource name.\" The `resourceNames` field works with subresources like `pods/exec`, but for list or watch operations, clients must include a matching `metadata.name` field selector.\n\nWildcards (`*`) can grant broad permissions across resources and verbs, though this approach risks unintended access to future resource types and subresources.\n\n## Aggregated ClusterRoles\n\nClusterRoles can aggregate rules from other ClusterRoles using an `aggregationRule` with label selectors. A control plane controller automatically populates the combined `rules` field based on matching ClusterRole objects.\n\nCritical warning: \"The control plane overwrites any values that you manually specify in the `rules` field of an aggregate ClusterRole.\" To modify aggregated rules, update the selected ClusterRole objects instead. Avoid including a `rules` field in aggregated ClusterRole manifests to prevent field manager conflicts.\n\n## Referring to Subjects\n\nBindings grant permissions to subjects—users, groups, or ServiceAccounts. Kubernetes represents usernames as strings (plain names, email-style, or numeric IDs). The `system:` prefix is reserved for system use and should not appear in custom user or group names.\n\nServiceAccounts follow a specific naming convention: usernames use `system:serviceaccount:` prefix, while group names use `system:serviceaccounts:` (plural).\n\n## Privilege Escalation Prevention and Bootstrapping\n\nTwo key restrictions prevent unauthorized privilege escalation:\n\n**Role creation/update**: Users can only create or modify roles if they possess all permissions the role grants (at the same scope) or have explicit `escalate` permission on roles/clusterroles in the `rbac.authorization.k8s.io` API group.\n\n**Role binding creation/update**: Users can only create bindings if they hold all permissions in the referenced role OR have explicit `bind` permission on that role.\n\n## Default Roles and Role Bindings\n\nAPI servers automatically create default ClusterRoles and ClusterRoleBindings, labeled with `kubernetes.io/bootstrapping=rbac-defaults`. At startup, the API server reconciles defaults, restoring missing permissions and subjects.\n\nTo disable auto-reconciliation, set the `rbac.authorization.kubernetes.io/autoupdate` annotation to `false` on default cluster roles or bindings, though this risks non-functional clusters.\n\n### Discovery Roles Table\n\n| Default ClusterRole | Default ClusterRoleBinding | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **system:basic-user** | **system:authenticated** group | Provides read-only access to basic self-information |\n| **system:discovery** | **system:authenticated** group | Allows read-only access to API discovery endpoints |\n| **system:public-info-viewer** | **system:authenticated** and **system:unauthenticated** groups | Provides read-only access to non-sensitive cluster information |\n\n### User-Facing Roles Table\n\n| Default ClusterRole | Default ClusterRoleBinding | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **cluster-admin** | **system:masters** group | Grants super-user access to any action on any resource. In ClusterRoleBindings, provides full control over all cluster resources and namespaces. In RoleBindings, gives full control within the binding's namespace. |\n| **admin** | None | Intended for namespace-level use via RoleBinding. Allows read/write access to most namespace resources and enables creating roles and bindings within that namespace. Does not permit write access to resource quotas, the namespace itself, or EndpointSlices in Kubernetes v1.22+. |\n| **edit** | None | Permits read/write access to most namespace objects without viewing or modifying roles and bindings. Allows accessing Secrets and running Pods as any ServiceAccount in the namespace. Does not allow EndpointSlice write access in Kubernetes v1.22+. |\n| **view** | None | Provides read-only access to most namespace objects, excluding roles, bindings, and Secrets. Hiding Secrets prevents potential privilege escalation through ServiceAccount credential exposure. |\n\n## Command-Line Utilities\n\n### kubectl create role\n\nGenerates a Role within a single namespace with specified permissions. Examples include creating roles for pod reading, resource-specific access, API group specification, subresource permissions, and instance-level permissions.\n\n### kubectl create clusterrole\n\nCreates a ClusterRole for cluster-wide permissions. Capabilities include pod reader roles, resource name restrictions, API group specification, subresource access, non-resource URLs (like `/logs/*`), and aggregation rules.\n\n### kubectl create rolebinding\n\nGrants namespace-scoped permissions from a Role or ClusterRole to users or ServiceAccounts. Bindings apply within a specific namespace.\n\n### kubectl create clusterrolebinding\n\nExtends ClusterRole permissions across the entire cluster to users or ServiceAccounts.\n\n### kubectl auth reconcile\n\nCreates or updates RBAC objects from manifest files, handling namespace creation and updating existing roles/bindings. The `--remove-extra-permissions` and `--remove-extra-subjects` flags clean up unintended access. The `--dry-run=client` option previews changes before applying.","durationMs":17386,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac.md"}}
