{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011yjj39gZa8VFtdbB5R4UhC","type":"tool_result","content":"# TUF: What It Is and What It Protects Against\n\n**What TUF Is:**\n\"a framework (a set of libraries, file formats, and utilities) that can be used to secure new and existing software update systems.\"\n\n**What It Protects Against:**\nTUF guards against attacks including:\n- \"An attacker keeps giving you the same file, so you never realize there is an update.\"\n- \"An attacker gives you an older, insecure version of a file that you already have and tricks you into thinking it's newer.\"\n- \"An attacker gives you a newer version of a file you have but it's still not the newest one.\"\n- \"An attacker compromises the key used to sign these files. Now you download a file that is properly signed, but is still malicious.\"\n\n**Roles and Metadata:**\nNOT ON PAGE (The overview references the Roles and metadata section but does not detail the specific roles or their functions on this page.)\n\n**Key Compromise Resilience, Delegation, and Thresholds:**\nNOT ON PAGE\n\n**Integration into Other Systems:**\n\"TUF identifies the updates, downloads them, and checks them against the metadata that it also downloads from the repository. If the downloaded target files are trustworthy, TUF hands them over to your software update system.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"bfcad003-294c-49ee-9f21-9539a3246aa2","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:27:38.962Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":23258,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# TUF: What It Is and What It Protects Against\n\n**What TUF Is:**\n\"a framework (a set of libraries, file formats, and utilities) that can be used to secure new and existing software update systems.\"\n\n**What It Protects Against:**\nTUF guards against attacks including:\n- \"An attacker keeps giving you the same file, so you never realize there is an update.\"\n- \"An attacker gives you an older, insecure version of a file that you already have and tricks you into thinking it's newer.\"\n- \"An attacker gives you a newer version of a file you have but it's still not the newest one.\"\n- \"An attacker compromises the key used to sign these files. Now you download a file that is properly signed, but is still malicious.\"\n\n**Roles and Metadata:**\nNOT ON PAGE (The overview references the Roles and metadata section but does not detail the specific roles or their functions on this page.)\n\n**Key Compromise Resilience, Delegation, and Thresholds:**\nNOT ON PAGE\n\n**Integration into Other Systems:**\n\"TUF identifies the updates, downloads them, and checks them against the metadata that it also downloads from the repository. If the downloaded target files are trustworthy, TUF hands them over to your software update system.\"","durationMs":18679,"url":"https://theupdateframework.io/docs/overview/"}}
