{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SQHQ9UxJwjRBBXYR67N1Sa","type":"tool_result","content":"# Policy as Code (PaC)\n\n**What it is:**\n\"Policy as Code is the practice of storing the definition of policies as one or more files in machine-readable and processable form.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nOrganizations implement numerous policies—such as security restrictions on secrets in source code or container permissions—that developers must verify. Manual policy compliance checking is resource-intensive and error-prone, struggling to keep pace with cloud native application demands.\n\n**How it helps:**\nCodifying policies enables consistent, automated enforcement while reducing human error. Version control systems like Git track policy changes, creating an audit trail and allowing teams to identify who implemented changes and revert if needed."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"899a98f0-b2d7-4424-a451-e8bb8709fb13","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:32:15.606Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":47732,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Policy as Code (PaC)\n\n**What it is:**\n\"Policy as Code is the practice of storing the definition of policies as one or more files in machine-readable and processable form.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nOrganizations implement numerous policies—such as security restrictions on secrets in source code or container permissions—that developers must verify. Manual policy compliance checking is resource-intensive and error-prone, struggling to keep pace with cloud native application demands.\n\n**How it helps:**\nCodifying policies enables consistent, automated enforcement while reducing human error. Version control systems like Git track policy changes, creating an audit trail and allowing teams to identify who implemented changes and revert if needed.","durationMs":2297,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/policy-as-code/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HuRxsbBpogeRDJPTSJ5kd8","type":"tool_result","content":"# Cloud Native Security\n\n**Definition:**\nCloud native security is an approach that integrates security throughout cloud native applications' lifecycles, from development to production, adapting traditional security models to environments with rapid code changes and ephemeral infrastructure.\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nTraditional security assumptions fail in cloud native contexts. Applications change frequently, depend on numerous open source libraries, run on vendor-controlled infrastructure, and experience rapid infrastructure shifts. Conventional practices like code reviews, extended QA cycles, and late-stage security reviews cannot scale with modern deployment velocities.\n\n**How it helps:**\nThis approach replaces manual audits with automated scanning integrated into release pipelines. Code gets scanned for vulnerabilities before compilation. Open source dependencies come from trusted sources with ongoing vulnerability monitoring. Rather than impeding change, cloud native security embraces it through frequent component updates and regular infrastructure replacement.\n\n**Related concept:**\nCloud native security is closely connected to [DevSecOps](/devsecops/), which embeds security practices across development and operations teams."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"992f301b-8d0a-470e-ab0c-c054b2f47769","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:32:17.958Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":49193,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Cloud Native Security\n\n**Definition:**\nCloud native security is an approach that integrates security throughout cloud native applications' lifecycles, from development to production, adapting traditional security models to environments with rapid code changes and ephemeral infrastructure.\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nTraditional security assumptions fail in cloud native contexts. Applications change frequently, depend on numerous open source libraries, run on vendor-controlled infrastructure, and experience rapid infrastructure shifts. Conventional practices like code reviews, extended QA cycles, and late-stage security reviews cannot scale with modern deployment velocities.\n\n**How it helps:**\nThis approach replaces manual audits with automated scanning integrated into release pipelines. Code gets scanned for vulnerabilities before compilation. Open source dependencies come from trusted sources with ongoing vulnerability monitoring. Rather than impeding change, cloud native security embraces it through frequent component updates and regular infrastructure replacement.\n\n**Related concept:**\nCloud native security is closely connected to [DevSecOps](/devsecops/), which embeds security practices across development and operations teams.","durationMs":3476,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/cloud-native-security/"}}
