{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VDi8VNwpCMPfz64UMo7KMv","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Secrets: Security Considerations\n\n## Default Storage Risk\n\nThe documentation emphasizes a critical security concern: \"Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store (etcd). Anyone with API access can retrieve or modify a Secret, and so can anyone with access to etcd.\"\n\nThe guidance recommends several protective steps:\n\n1. Enable encryption at rest for Secrets\n2. Configure RBAC rules with least-privilege access\n3. Restrict Secret access to specific containers\n4. Consider external Secret store providers\n\n## Information Security Protections\n\nKubernetes applies additional protections to Secrets beyond what ConfigMaps receive. The system implements several safeguards: secrets are only transmitted to nodes when Pods require them, the kubelet stores copies in temporary filesystems rather than persistent storage, and once dependent Pods are deleted, local copies are removed.\n\nAuthorization controls are significant—granting list or watch permissions on Secrets allows reading all Secret data in a namespace, not just explicitly referenced ones. Access should be restricted to minimum necessary permissions, and broad roles like cluster-admin should be avoided unless administratively required.\n\nA notable security limitation exists: containers running with `privileged: true` can access all Secrets on that node.\n\n## Environment Variables vs. Volume Mounts\n\nThe documentation does not directly compare exposure risks between these methods or explicitly state that environment variables may be revealed through logs or crash reports. However, it notes that when Secrets are mounted as volumes, updates propagate automatically to Pods, whereas \"a container using a Secret as a subPath volume mount does not receive automated Secret updates.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","uuid":"7a9d1102-d2ef-4b24-a035-ed677dc68858","timestamp":"2026-08-31T04:33:04.396Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":32981,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Secrets: Security Considerations\n\n## Default Storage Risk\n\nThe documentation emphasizes a critical security concern: \"Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store (etcd). Anyone with API access can retrieve or modify a Secret, and so can anyone with access to etcd.\"\n\nThe guidance recommends several protective steps:\n\n1. Enable encryption at rest for Secrets\n2. Configure RBAC rules with least-privilege access\n3. Restrict Secret access to specific containers\n4. Consider external Secret store providers\n\n## Information Security Protections\n\nKubernetes applies additional protections to Secrets beyond what ConfigMaps receive. The system implements several safeguards: secrets are only transmitted to nodes when Pods require them, the kubelet stores copies in temporary filesystems rather than persistent storage, and once dependent Pods are deleted, local copies are removed.\n\nAuthorization controls are significant—granting list or watch permissions on Secrets allows reading all Secret data in a namespace, not just explicitly referenced ones. Access should be restricted to minimum necessary permissions, and broad roles like cluster-admin should be avoided unless administratively required.\n\nA notable security limitation exists: containers running with `privileged: true` can access all Secrets on that node.\n\n## Environment Variables vs. Volume Mounts\n\nThe documentation does not directly compare exposure risks between these methods or explicitly state that environment variables may be revealed through logs or crash reports. However, it notes that when Secrets are mounted as volumes, updates propagate automatically to Pods, whereas \"a container using a Secret as a subPath volume mount does not receive automated Secret updates.\"","durationMs":5195,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/configuration/secret.md"}}
