{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Pods and containers (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/linux-kernel-security-constraints/)\n\nTranscribed from the page's markdown source. Code blocks omitted; prose is the source's own.\n\n## Run workloads without root"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"2dd554a2-c2e3-4c47-b439-296e16828990"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" privileges\n\n\"When you deploy a workload in Kubernetes, use the Pod specification to restrict that workload from running as the root user on the node. You can use the Pod `securityContext` to define the specific Linux user and group for the processes in the Pod, and"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9f972efe-13de-4d10-a575-7924c8154361"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" explicitly restrict containers from running as root users. Setting these values in the Pod manifest takes precedence over similar values in the container image, which is especially useful if you're running images that you don't own.\"\n\n\"Ensure that the user or group that you"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"2588724b-1598-44fb-a7b4-cabf16e9a7fa"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" assign to the workload has the permissions required for the application to function correctly. Changing the user or group to one that doesn't have the correct permissions could lead to file access issues or failed operations.\"\n\n\"Configuring the kernel security features on this"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"8eb1f5e2-56d3-49f6-9aae-e506f2c551a0"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" page provides fine-grained control over the actions that processes in your cluster can take, but managing these configurations can be challenging at scale. Running containers as non-root, or in user namespaces if you need root privileges, helps to"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"7bd47a4c-4542-48f6-bc13-78c5609387de"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" reduce the chance that you'll need to enforce your configured kernel security capabilities.\"\n\n## Security features in the Linux kernel\n\n\"Kubernetes lets you configure and use Linux kernel features to improve isolation and harden your containerized workloads"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"404b2949-40d3-42bf-9a50-c11bf2c3851d"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":". Common features include the following:\n\n* **Secure computing mode (seccomp)**: Filter which system calls a process can make\n* **AppArmor**: Restrict the access privileges of individual programs\n* **Security Enhanced Linux (SELinux)**: Ass"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"d08038f2-7501-423e-b1ed-dced6cc41928"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ign security labels to objects for more manageable security policy enforcement\"\n\n\"To configure settings for one of these features, the operating system that you choose for your nodes must enable the feature in the kernel. For example, Ubuntu 7.10 and later enable AppArmor by default."}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"11cf3866-0b5c-4ce0-9146-7de20f33d40e"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" To learn whether your OS enables a specific feature, consult the OS documentation.\"\n\n\"You use the `securityContext` field in your Pod specification to define the constraints that apply to those processes. The `securityContext` field also supports other security settings, such as specific Linux cap"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"8da6a97a-82a9-4693-8379-f4ca9582bee5"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"abilities or file access permissions using UIDs and GIDs.\"\n\n### seccomp\n\n\"Some of your workloads might need privileges to perform specific actions as the root user on your node's host machine. Linux uses capabilities to div"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"d17ac578-ec2d-4fcc-abe0-97968c22b171"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ide the available privileges into categories, so that processes can get the privileges required to perform specific actions without being granted all privileges. Each capability has a set of system calls (syscalls) that a process can make. seccomp lets you restrict these"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"86aa00ed-29dc-44ed-9650-17689778b1cb"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" individual syscalls. It can be used to sandbox the privileges of a process, restricting the calls it is able to make from userspace into the kernel.\"\n\n\"In Kubernetes, you use a *container runtime* on each node"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"1c098d52-1409-46c8-950d-a3af38b589b3"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" to run your containers. Example runtimes include CRI-O, Docker, or containerd. Each runtime allows only a subset of Linux capabilities by default. You can further limit the allowed syscalls individually by using a seccomp profile. Container runtimes us"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"87c19726-0228-4e25-83da-51265b60ba1a"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ually include a default seccomp profile. Kubernetes lets you automatically apply seccomp profiles loaded onto a node to your Pods and containers.\"\n\n\"Kubernetes also has the `allowPrivilegeEscalation` setting for Pods and containers. When set"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"c65abbd4-088a-44e1-810b-ad3899310c99"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" to `false`, this prevents processes from gaining new capabilities and restricts unprivileged users from changing the applied seccomp profile to a more permissive profile.\"\n\n#### Considerations for seccomp\n\n\"seccomp is a low"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"1a58810f-81f0-4b6b-89b9-2f6dd20e1508"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"-level security configuration that you should only configure yourself if you require fine-grained control over Linux syscalls. Using seccomp, especially at scale, has the following risks:\n\n* Configurations might break during application updates\n* Attackers can still use all"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"5da1182a-d669-4046-8e9c-83b9654d7a0d"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"owed syscalls to exploit vulnerabilities\n* Profile management for individual applications becomes challenging at scale\"\n\n\"Recommendation: Use the default seccomp profile that's bundled with your container runtime. If you need a"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"82feac77-d63d-44b0-8abb-4e94329c98eb"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" more isolated environment, consider using a sandbox, such as gVisor. Sandboxes solve the preceding risks with custom seccomp profiles, but require more compute resources on your nodes and might have compatibility issues with G"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"3b33afb1-9f0a-4a9b-9af8-2cce69322a22"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"PUs and other specialized hardware.\"\n\n### AppArmor and SELinux: policy-based mandatory access control\n\n\"You can use Linux policy-based mandatory access control (MAC) mechanisms, such as AppArmor and SELinux, to harden your Kubernetes"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"49a6376b-a399-4b46-a20a-1dff8a0b986a"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" workloads.\"\n\n#### AppArmor\n\n\"AppArmor is a Linux kernel security module that supplements the standard Linux user and group based permissions to confine programs to a limited set of resources. AppArmor can"}},"session_id":"bf938bdb-ceb6-4379-83e1-38e2e5739719","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"50eb7cc1-0a69-46bb-808b-cddb549aa97f"}
